<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Four Noble Truths</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Catvāryāryasatyāni, Cattāri ariyasaccāni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Four Noble Truths are a central teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>: dukkha, usually rendered suffering or unsatisfactoriness; its origin in craving and attachment; its cessation, or nirvana; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noble-eightfold-path">Noble Eightfold Path</a> leading to that cessation. Buddhist tradition identifies them as the Buddha's first teaching, set out in the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta. Some scholars hold that this identification was made later and that the formulation developed over time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> tradition gave them great importance by about the 5th century CE, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> places more weight on emptiness and the bodhisattva path. Western presentations since the 19th century often present them as the central teaching of Buddhism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/four-noble-truths.jpg" alt="Four Noble Truths" width="330" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asia Society created the file. Artwork created by an anonymous ancient source. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astasahasrika_Prajnaparamita_Dharmacakra_Discourse.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Pali term ariya sacca, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> arya satya, is commonly translated as noble truths, a convention started by the earliest English translators. The basic set, according to the philologist K. R. Norman, runs: this is pain, this is the origin of pain, this is the cessation of pain, and this is the path leading to the cessation of pain.</p><p>Dukkha is a quality of transient existence. Sensory contact gives rise to craving and clinging to impermanent things, and this craving keeps people in samsara, usually understood as the cycle of rebirth. The cycle ends with nirvana, the cessation of craving, reached by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noble-eightfold-path">Noble Eightfold Path</a> of restraint, discipline and wholesome states, mindfulness and meditation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The truths are best known from the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, which contains two sets of them. The commonly used full set contains grammatical errors, pointing to multiple sources, but the Pali tradition considered it correct and did not change it. In the Mahasaccaka Sutta the Buddha attains three knowledges, the last of them knowledge of the destruction of the taints, which is the knowledge of the four truths. Since the 19th century scholars have argued that the formulation was a later development, a proposal not settled.</p><p>Léon Feer suggested in 1870 that the truths entered Buddhist literature through the vinaya collections. In 1935 Caroline Rhys Davids noted that they are missing from the Book of the Fours of the Aṅguttara Nikāya, and in the 1960s André Bareau argued that they were not part of the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, since important versions lack them. Carol S. Anderson holds that the tradition slowly recognised them as the Buddha's first teaching, after liberating insight came to be seen as liberating in itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Anderson proposes that the truths became central to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> by about the 5th century CE, with the composition of the commentaries, and the Theravada tradition regards insight into them as liberating in itself. They are less prominent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a>, which centres insight into emptiness and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">Bodhisattva</a> path. From the 19th century, with Western colonial exploration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and Buddhist modernism, they were often presented in the West as the central teaching of Buddhism. Anderson sees a simplification tied to the colonial project of gaining control over Buddhism. B. R. Ambedkar's Navayana rejected the Four Noble Truths, calling them, according to him, the invention of wrong-headed monks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Four Noble Truths” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The core of the Buddha’s teaching, as the early texts present it, is the <b>Four Noble Truths</b>: life is marked by suffering or unsatisfactoriness (dukkha); suffering arises from craving; craving can cease; and there is a path that leads to its cessation.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
